    {
      "filename": "Small-Flowered Sand Verbena, Great Sand Dunes.jpg",
      "title": "File:Small-Flowered Sand Verbena, Great Sand Dunes.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Small-Flowered_Sand_Verbena%2C_Great_Sand_Dunes.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small-Flowered_Sand_Verbena,_Great_Sand_Dunes.jpg",
      "width": 4608,
      "height": 3456,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Great Sand Dunes National Park and Preserve",
      "description": "NPS/Patrick Myers\nThis rare plant generates many visitor questions each summer. Visitors assume the large, lush seedpod is a blossom, but the flowers are actually the tiny white blossoms at the end of succulent, pink tubes. \nThis verbena looks like it belongs more in a tropical rainforest than a desert - just another hidden surprise at Great Sand Dunes!\n\nThe scientific name for this species is Tripterocalyx micranthus. Because this plant is relatively rare, its historical medicinal uses are not well-known. Regional tribes used related Tripterocalyx species as a diaphoretic, sedative, snake bite treatment, and sore throat treatment.",
